$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM benzyl (2-methylallyl)carbamate (1b). To a flame dried flask under argon
was added using 2-methylprop-2-en-1-ol 10a (576 mg, 8 mmol, 1 equiv.),
DBU (2.27 mL, 15.2 mmol, 1.9 equiv.) and toluene (20 mL, 0.4M). The flask
was cooled to 0 °C and DPPA (3.1 mL, 14.4 mmol, 1.8 equiv.) was added
dropwise over 10 minutes. The reaction was kept at 0 °C for 30 minutes
and then allowed to warm to room temperature and stirred overnight. The
reaction was then diluted with a mixture of THF:H2O (12.5mL:5mL),
followed by the dropwise addition of a 1M solution of PMe3 in Toluene (8
mL, 1 equiv.). The reaction was stirred for 4h after which NEt3 (1.34 mL,
9.6 mmol, 1.2 equiv.), and CbzCl (1.37 mL, 9.6 mmol, 1.2 equiv.) were
added and the solution was stirred for and additional 16h. The reaction
was diluted with ethyl acetate (100 mL) and brine (50 mL) and the organic
layer was removed. The aqueous layer was extracted with ethyl acetate
(4X100 mL). The organic layers were combined and washed with brine (2X50
mL) and NaHCO3 (aq), dried with MgSO4, and the solvent was evaporated.
The crude material was then purified by column chromatography eluting
with 10% to 20% ethyl actate:pet ether. A colorless oil (1.36 g, 83%
yield). Spectral data was consistent with the literature4.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Ruthenium-Catalyzed Alkene-Alkyne Coupling of Disubstituted Olefins:
Application to the Stereoselective Synthesis of Trisubstituted
Enecarbamates
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Trost, Barry M.; Cregg, James J.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2015), 137(2), 620-623

